 {:db/id "q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "8915c372-42a6-5420-8c91-5f069925b152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c/5da860ce5b7b7ed37c76c31dc8fafa6a5b225d7b3b10e14684a01dcf9911035a" "q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c/11b05c35596d9e18e72f77c033ca49f1ba95958751fb53b5bbf7d88fcaf3cae6" "q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c/8f9bf5f216bf38a64a782e3e66e18dca698f56574da5254aaad75723dfe15ab6" "q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c/ed392badef89680c0ca4111d46e4b1c749ee18bad31d9686d6eebb83cdf23013" "q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c/beb69334c8e750d5104024ab13d4958d11f7a189c8e9a9ec7f6146d7abeff65e"] :answer-field/correct "q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c/beb69334c8e750d5104024ab13d4958d11f7a189c8e9a9ec7f6146d7abeff65e" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-335312" :question/id #uuid "28461e48-9c29-58e8-b99d-2d64a173813c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335312" :question/problem "A rock of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the rock's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the rock is proportional to the square of the rock's velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the rock are the weight due to gravity and drag." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the rock:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the rock, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the rock (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the rock is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=2g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the rock, we get\n\n$$\n2g-k{v}^{2}=2\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335312/ma-source-field/ff77af3b74aaaf0b05efcfc4f56e41ab57d4054cefab2188b9be56e6d5d9be3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
