 {:db/id "q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5/e6e130125e5e6b9fa1e52b97a9d8bab730c54f22c254c81f9842acafb52afbb2" "q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5/c775df2fa72d1db0590378ab1c299724678e7159a6b17f280bb328c1dc42706a" "q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5/16c063b0839f83564b52fe4ada90fbe17658fc1c901af2ae6b0a9bf0fe4e0b66" "q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5/ccfc42d07bc1aa112214dd63d0c92548d9ac26776c857b29a5c1e48a177a2b42" "q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5/ff76e476fb7b315085c3d60065bed62e5de7161cba7290babdf1ad4728ba84f5"] :answer-field/correct "q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5/ccfc42d07bc1aa112214dd63d0c92548d9ac26776c857b29a5c1e48a177a2b42" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-335590" :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-335590/ma-source-field/d599ae7e9b1d60b0ace38c370cb5fbb4ef84dddc0fc4929b26d3f75ae48917d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
