 {:db/id "q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "151341a6-4b50-5d9d-ac35-c33852fb4d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6/c72838f5e065f2fca80b6b020e5aaffbe0a12d4dcf6ca0911c94e4f45447bcc7" "q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6/b7b12dfbc9b26d5d41501a2332b628538150ace54a6b3962cb454a750a92440d" "q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6/d6b66fcfc5e444ac4a6bc916d3c5792f7916cd83ee3c02d2fb20a2501d6fa39d" "q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6/23a1d7025ecf342598ad3e09f648c0c09c833202e4c753ebb43af5ba7b406ff5" "q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6/2eb457185f935f968490ef1216f8b0df90fd5575ca9c4427d6b4265a52cbaefd"] :answer-field/correct "q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6/23a1d7025ecf342598ad3e09f648c0c09c833202e4c753ebb43af5ba7b406ff5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-335639" :question/id #uuid "cb0dc9ee-4198-5fbc-84d6-225dac676eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335639/ma-source-field/3526d6bdb0d8a2d003d9b5f0cf176cc2d9d59185a2ae5ed0f42b5cd59867a7b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
