 {:db/id "q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a" :answer-field/id #uuid "02478602-b3c1-5c9d-81bf-ab4c5f0d8364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a/d10cc4f8a2a9045cb3384dcf6bebd2ab7de34f216ea0fcac11440a7b9ad5e4b0" "q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a/31a6566a9ca2f0de2ebb49093daf8d9793c1dfd279001cc1d8796ff6da45e271" "q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a/88e87d4bb3ab01b53f0372c9de2d4e2742ff2efb7545fe3b6d24647a13b96f56" "q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a/23a1d7025ecf342598ad3e09f648c0c09c833202e4c753ebb43af5ba7b406ff5" "q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a/1a52e3e199f19819eb2a1f11ea511bfa60715e9c8a2f8b6d8be1ade500cd6ca2"] :answer-field/correct "q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a/23a1d7025ecf342598ad3e09f648c0c09c833202e4c753ebb43af5ba7b406ff5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-335642" :question/id #uuid "53ed55d4-8280-5930-99c5-60558f8b8c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-335642" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6y+5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335642/ma-source-field/42043ed7cdea9fddeab8bccc6324ec851376bf16019c3db083d480fa3d926c8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
