 {:db/id "q-335673" :question/id #uuid "bbc98d60-8975-59af-8c09-070d79da0e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-335673" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n$$J(x^{\\ast})=\\begin{bmatrix}{{field-1}}&{{field-2}}\\\\{{field-3}}&{{field-4}}\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=2-8x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-8x-y & -x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-8(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335673/ma-source-field/a64208e8852da9dcf75f72458abf995a91c589513b9844664dc35376e3dbd3b8" "q-335673/ma-source-field/74c669c457397e8b4ee7d1665c6572fca41b2844fb17cfc1a9d8a73d4fec5663" "q-335673/ma-source-field/e6c2bbb05ed