 {:db/id "q-338587" :question/id #uuid "a42fad0a-c170-522e-8f97-0f501aa9bc73" :question/math-academy-id "q-338587" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nI. $4{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=1$\nII. ${y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,y(3)=2$\nIII. ${y}^{″}={x}^{3},\\,y(2)=5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition.\n\n- The second system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=3$).\n\n- The third system is not a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation combined with only one condition." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338587/ma-source-field/86b9616d384ec9558c8a91d0d80979eec27feac1ec10270d39e7c018143d24d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
