 {:db/id "q-339222" :question/id #uuid "b562af2b-8343-5000-806c-d01887adea11" :question/math-academy-id "q-339222" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $N(t)$ to an e-learning platform varies according to the differential equation ${N}^{′}(t)=rN(t),$ where $t$ is the time since the platform was launched, measured in weeks, and $r>0.$ It is known that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks. How long does it take for the number of subscribers to triple after the platform is launched? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the number of subscribers when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the number of subscribers equals $3{N}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N({t}_{1}) & =3{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=3{N}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the number of subscribers doubles after the first four weeks the platform is launched. Therefore,\n\n$$\nN(4)=2{N}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{N}_{0}{e}^{4r}=2{N}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}_{0}{e}^{4r} & =2{N}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{N}_{0}}{e}^{4r} & =2\\cancel{{N}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{4r} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈6.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339222/ma-source-field/816ba1edfe3e55d4dadabb3d03bb6e3f32351730480b66c55c64c87d4299dbf0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
