 {:db/id "q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa46205e-ab90-5810-a697-993aab9f4c54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9/8fa5c81f08795dc0fc058766b454fdfcfb72317769aebd1471d94507819b83c0" "q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9/cda4e09f3ca8772f220df0a589ea79b0bb6353de80a5e1f9c4fe92de70271f5e" "q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9/b6b8919febb64cc92a7cf7d8ed4d39a00e73b3521fcc55c836b113f1988aac98" "q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9/6ece2d8ec6a5b9bd9a3bb4f7d480c2ec598da67c532326d348f1a0a944b80df3" "q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9/ffd49047ff447eded16e0c618c5c46f6b627522d9e495463a66ebf860c3a3b7c"] :answer-field/correct "q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9/b6b8919febb64cc92a7cf7d8ed4d39a00e73b3521fcc55c836b113f1988aac98" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-339226" :question/id #uuid "8375c335-8cf2-5c82-b2c9-770890e7d65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339226" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of a tech device varies according to the differential equation ${V}^{′}(t)=-rV(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. How long does it take for the value to reduce to one-fifth of its original value? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the value when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the value equals $\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r}=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -r & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & ≈2.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-339226/ma-source-field/1a62824b16f59ceaa00b6f8dd311f55682bad5a86fd6d700cb85375ec554bbc9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
