 {:db/id "q-339251" :question/id #uuid "c104c7c7-be1b-5f7c-9495-548d3d354240" :question/math-academy-id "q-339251" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,y(4)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=-1$\nII. $x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=9$\nIII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-8x{y}^{′}+16y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=4$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=2$)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339251/ma-source-field/d1ba8c2d546dde00e949d026a20a7c5b6abe8e5e4a53358346933211a1bc773b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
