 {:db/id "q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "91d07a7f-1c6a-5d61-8b5b-d831b5a92a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1/48675f76701eb39f1edb8e8081213521d49acd2f4faaaba166fe3671ae23b745" "q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1/48dd138d5ce327f0640104c5aa43435007410fb7d41a3b9342d6ad2addc22350" "q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1/bc6ba6b1e329669a3de4ca3b2ec8b2596cde7ded21cefeb0c45f25b4836719ba" "q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1/4f26566ea3f7894d2fdc2a61a8a5b0b899d546a578fe1b979091f81e5716b6aa" "q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1/c3021239fbe88d6eee980e20a3756afa8e3fd5e2316e26c6dd314a4a4b9ca0cb"] :answer-field/correct "q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1/48675f76701eb39f1edb8e8081213521d49acd2f4faaaba166fe3671ae23b745" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-345668" :question/id #uuid "a1f58e5d-5f27-5c62-b42a-8d5fcfc6f214" :question/math-academy-id "q-345668" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-5)x{y}^{′}+(5+2x)y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(16\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{5}{16},1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345668/ma-source-field/1cd207816a3295f0c5b9c3693110c63b1be0da781f1a3989ffbe8d5b0027e0f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
