 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "49b0818a-b11e-5262-8180-2b40dd5a4460"] :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-35429/ma-source-field/326bb13c2d15a7f15b4bc9e37e9b44ff0406fd4f364edfedc29f3540922719e2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
