 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "863f05c5-808b-57a4-8854-1d3c4552848f"] :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-35509/ma-source-field/09a9ec2ca21ec49147f83b24a823cf71b30d4e8dde777af3d8a3b0cdbce9fe3a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
